# EVO-T1 Coder stack — canonical environment template.
# Copy to .env (bootstrap.sh does this) and edit. Never commit .env.

# --- Coder ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Host port for the Coder UI. Kasm owns 3000, so Coder uses 3001.
CODER_HTTP_PORT=3001
# Second path to Coder's plain listener, for workspace agents dialing
# host.docker.internal (see coder_agent_url in templates/docker-dev).
CODER_AGENT_TUNNEL_PORT=3002
# URL other machines use to reach Coder. Workspaces call back through this
# URL, so it must be a LAN-routable address — bootstrap.sh fills in your LAN
# IP when it can.
# Must be https: the Coder frontend calls crypto.randomUUID(), which browsers
# expose only in secure contexts, so an http:// LAN address breaks the UI. The
# coder container serves TLS itself with a self-signed cert bootstrap.sh
# issues, so a browser warning on the first visit is expected.
CODER_ACCESS_URL=https://YOUR_LAN_IP:3001
# Postgres (Coder's database).
CODER_PG_PASSWORD=change-me-postgres
# Prometheus metrics for the Coder control plane, published on 127.0.0.1 only.
# Point a scraper at http://127.0.0.1:<port>/metrics on the stack host.
CODER_METRICS_PORT=2112
# Browser session and CLI/API-key lifetimes. The product defaults (24h / 7d)
# log the coder CLI and the IDE extensions out weekly, which reads as a bug on
# a single-user box. Accepts Go durations (720h = 30 days).
CODER_SESSION_DURATION=720h
CODER_DEFAULT_TOKEN_LIFETIME=720h
# The Coder server refuses to boot unless the OAuth refresh lifetime is STRICTLY
# greater than CODER_SESSION_DURATION (it crash-loops with "default OAuth refresh
# lifetime ... must be strictly greater than session duration"). Keep this above
# CODER_SESSION_DURATION if you raise it. Maps to CODER_DEFAULT_OAUTH_REFRESH_LIFETIME.
CODER_OAUTH_REFRESH_LIFETIME=8760h
# Coder marks the deployment unhealthy after 5 slow median-query probes. The
# 15ms default is realistic to trip when Postgres contends for I/O with a
# loaded model on this box, so the threshold is raised.
CODER_HEALTH_CHECK_THRESHOLD_DATABASE=60ms
# Quiet hours clamp autostop deadlines, so the timezone matters: leaving this
# at UTC force-stops workspaces at the wrong wall-clock hour. Use a
# CRON_TZ=<Area/City> cron expression. Quoted because scripts source .env.
CODER_QUIET_HOURS="CRON_TZ=UTC 0 3 * * *"
# The built-in DERP STUN servers default to Google endpoints, which buy nothing
# on a LAN. Keep `disable`; agents connect directly over WireGuard and DERP
# remains the fallback relay.
CODER_DERP_STUN=disable
# Experiment opt-ins for the Coder server, comma-separated; empty = none
# (safe, the default). `agents` is NOT an experiment name in 2.36.6 — the
# server ignored it with an "ignoring unknown experiment" warning on every
# boot (verified against codersdk/deployment.go ExperimentsKnown at
# v2.36.6). Coder Agents gates on the entitlement feature
# `workspace_external_agent`, which this key does not unlock on a CE
# install; real experiment names (chat-advisor, chat-virtual-desktop, ...)
# are listed by `coder server --help`.
CODER_EXPERIMENTS=

# Default for the template variable `grok_profile_mode`, which the create form also
# shows per workspace as the `Grok profile` dropdown (override it there for one
# workspace; that needs a rebuild, like `dind`). Read once per push, so changing this
# default needs a re-push. `shared` = every workspace of a user mounts that user's
# shared grok-profile volume nested inside /home/coder, so config.toml, the MCP warm
# caches, memory-v2, skills and a self-installed CLI are one profile shared by that
# account's workspaces, re-rendered on every start (last-started workspace wins).
# `private` = no nested mount: each workspace gets a private ~/.grok on its own home
# volume, so its config.toml (plus memory, skills and logs) differ per workspace and
# die with the workspace. A workspace moving shared -> private starts on an empty
# profile: nothing is copied and the shared volume is left alone.
# See README "Grok Build state: one shared profile per user".
GROK_PROFILE_MODE=shared

# PREFERRED GitHub credential path: Coder's external-auth broker. Nothing to
# set here — the CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_* keys do NOT belong in this file. A
# defined one counts as a configured provider even when empty, and an empty
# key makes the coder server exit at boot ("external auth provider \"\"
# doesn't have a valid id"), which the restart policy turns into a crash
# loop. To enable the broker, create coder.env next to docker-compose.yml
# (gitignored; restart to apply) with ALL keys filled — ID and TYPE both name
# the provider "github"; leave the file absent for no broker at all:
#   CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID=github
#   CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_TYPE=github
#   CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_CLIENT_ID=<GitHub OAuth App client id>
#   CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_CLIENT_SECRET=<GitHub OAuth App client secret>
#   CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_REVOKE_URL=https://github.com/settings/connections/applications/<client id>
#   #CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_REGEX=github\.com  (only to tell providers/hosts
#   apart; omitted matches every github.com remote)
# Then connect your GitHub account in Coder (Settings -> Tokens /
# external auth). Workspaces then mint SHORT-LIVED, per-host tokens on demand —
# nothing long-lived lives in any file, env or template, and one connection
# covers every workspace, which is why this replaces pasting a PAT below.
# The callback URL on the GitHub side is <CODER_ACCESS_URL>/external-auth/github/callback
# and the App installation (or token grant) must include the repos you clone:
# a connected-but-unscoped credential authenticates and is still denied (403).

# Fallback only: a long-lived fine-grained PAT, forwarded as
# `--var github_token=` to scripts/push-template.sh only when it is non-empty. When
# set it reaches every workspace as the GH_TOKEN and GITHUB_TOKEN environment
# variables, so `git push`, `gh pr create` and `gh api` work from a workspace
# terminal and from Grok Build's Bash tool (gh is in the golden image and reads
# GH_TOKEN). Env-only, like LITELLM_API_KEY: the token is never written to a file,
# and an empty value here means no GitHub credential exists anywhere. It is a WRITE
# credential for whatever the token can reach. This is the FALLBACK path — prefer
# the broker block above. If you do paste a PAT here, scope it to exactly the
# repos you clone, rotate it when work ends, and keep it out of shell history,
# diffs and anything committed. (A token without a resource grant authenticates
# and is still DENIED on private repos — that, not the token format, is the
# failure mode to check first when a clone or push is denied.)
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# With GITHUB_TOKEN set, adds the github MCP HTTP server
# (https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/, authenticated by that token) to every
# workspace's rendered ~/.grok/config.toml, so Grok Build gets GitHub tools as well
# as the gh CLI. Off unless GITHUB_TOKEN is set too; leave it off unless you want
# Grok to drive GitHub tools.
GITHUB_MCP=

# --- LiteLLM --------------------------------------------------------------
LITELLM_PORT=4000
LITELLM_MASTER_KEY=change-me-litellm
# Optional NVIDIA Spark boxes — Ollama endpoints behind the spark-* aliases.
# Placeholder hostnames on purpose; replace with real Ollama URLs or leave
# them as-is (those aliases simply fail when used).
SPARK1_OLLAMA_URL=http://spark-1.lan:11434
SPARK2_OLLAMA_URL=http://spark-2.lan:11434
# OpenAI-compatible /v1 endpoints for the ONE `agent` model group: three
# deployments, one per Spark box, and `least-busy` routing sends each request to
# whichever deployment has the fewest requests in flight. Grok Build / Cline /
# Roo need structured tool calls, which the Arc IPEX-LLM Ollama returns as plain
# text for the Qwen2.5-Coder weights; `coder` and `coder-fast` stay useful for
# completion and fill-in work, and `chat` (qwen2.5:7b) does emit real tool
# calls. Leaving all three URLs empty keeps `agent` defined but unusable —
# LiteLLM then falls back to `coder`, the Arc alias.
SPARK1_OPENAI_URL=http://spark-1.lan:8888/v1
SPARK2_OPENAI_URL=http://spark-2.lan:8888/v1
SPARK3_OPENAI_URL=http://spark-3.lan:8888/v1
# Bearer tokens for the three OpenAI-compatible endpoints above. TensorFold is
# vLLM-lineage, so treat it like vLLM/SGLang: any non-empty string is accepted
# unless the server was started with --api-key — then set the REAL key per box
# here. Empty is not a working value: LiteLLM v1.102.1 fails every request at
# client construction (AuthenticationError, before reaching the box) when the
# key is empty, and `agent` falls back to `coder`. The placeholder `local-spark`
# is not a secret and is ignored by a keyless backend.
SPARK_1_API_KEY=local-spark
SPARK_2_API_KEY=local-spark
SPARK_3_API_KEY=local-spark
# Per-box health probes for the dashboard's Spark cards (one widget per box, so
# no card assumes one "usual" box). Placeholder hostnames on purpose: compose
# already defaults each to http://spark-<n>.lan:8888/health, so these are
# overrides for real hostnames, not requirements.
# SPARK1_HEALTH_URL=http://spark-1.lan:8888/health
# SPARK2_HEALTH_URL=http://spark-2.lan:8888/health
# SPARK3_HEALTH_URL=http://spark-3.lan:8888/health
# Full /v1/models URL for the dashboard's "Agent backend" card — the card is the
# live signal for whether agent mode has a tool-capable route or has silently
# degraded to the text-only Arc fallback. All three Spark boxes serve the same
# model id, so point this at any reachable one (spark-2 by default); the per-box
# SPARKn_HEALTH_URL keys above are what tell you which box answered. Both the
# browser link and the status probe use this single URL.
AGENT_MODEL_URL=http://spark-2.lan:8888/v1/models

# --- Workspace golden image ----------------------------------------------
# Built once by scripts/build-dev-image.sh; referenced by name from the Coder
# docker provider, so it resolves from the local image store and never pulls.
# Build it before creating a workspace, or change this to a Coder base image.
# bootstrap.sh builds it when it is missing and then pushes the template via
# scripts/push-template.sh.
DEV_IMAGE=evo-t1-dev:latest
# Optional default for the create form's Git repository field (both templates).
# A non-empty URL prefills that field; empty keeps each template's own default
# (docker-dev = no clone, docker-devcontainer = the coder/coder demo repo). The
# clone itself runs inside the workspace at start; per-workspace URLs go in the
# form's Git repository field, or use scripts/new-workspace.sh <git-url>.
REPO_URL=
# Docker-in-docker is NOT set here: the toggle is the "Docker-in-docker"
# Yes/No question on the Coder create form (preset "AI workspace + DinD"),
# default off, fixed at create time. When on it needs the daemon image
# docker:29.8.1-dind in the host image store — bootstrap.sh pulls it, or run
# `docker pull docker:29.8.1-dind` by hand. Terraform never pulls it, so a
# missing image fails the workspace plan with "did not find docker image".
# SKIP_DEV_IMAGE_BUILD=1 and SKIP_TEMPLATE_PUSH=1 restore the old print-only
# behaviour of bootstrap.sh; SKIP_SPARKS=1 skips the Spark /v1/models probe.
# bootstrap.sh reads them from the process environment only, never from this
# file, so uncommenting them here does nothing: export them, or run
# `SKIP_TEMPLATE_PUSH=1 ./scripts/bootstrap.sh`.

# --- Ollama (IPEX-LLM on the Arc 140T) -------------------------------------
# Models pulled by scripts/pull-models.sh (first run is a big download).
OLLAMA_MODELS="qwen2.5-coder:32b qwen2.5-coder:14b qwen2.5:7b"

# --- Kasm -----------------------------------------------------------------
# 3000: first-boot install wizard. 4443: Kasm UI after the wizard.
KASM_WIZARD_PORT=3000
KASM_UI_PORT=4443

# --- Homepage dashboard --------------------------------------------------
# The dashboard container publishes nothing; an nginx sidecar owns the default
# web ports. 80 redirects to 443, which terminates TLS with the self-signed cert
# bootstrap.sh writes into proxy/certs/ (gitignored) and proxies to the
# dashboard over the compose network.
HOMEPAGE_HTTP_PORT=80
HOMEPAGE_HTTPS_PORT=443
# The router sidecar's plain-HTTP port for the public subdomains; the main
# Traefik points its routers at this (proxy/router.conf routes by Host).
STACK_PROXY_HTTP_PORT=8080
# Host the browser uses to reach the dashboard. bootstrap.sh derives it from
# CODER_ACCESS_URL, so the dashboard's allowed-host list and its links to Coder,
# LiteLLM and Kasm all track the same address. Leave empty to fall back to
# localhost (only useful from the machine itself).
STACK_LAN_HOST=
# Public access, off by default: leave this empty and everything below stays
# LAN-only. Set it to the comma-separated public names, in the form this stack
# was installed with:
#   STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=gmktecbeast.overeazy.io,coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io,kasm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io,litellm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io
# Each service name is ONE label under the zone and carries its prefix with a
# HYPHEN. A wildcard certificate covers exactly one label, so the dotted
# two-label form (coder.gmktecbeast.) has no certificate at the public edge and
# the browser fails with ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH before Traefik sees
# the request. Keep the hyphen when adding a service.
# Entries are DNS names, not URLs (no scheme, no port, no path). bootstrap.sh
# derives STACK_DASHBOARD_URL / STACK_CODER_URL / STACK_KASM_URL /
# STACK_LITELLM_URL from the prefix on each entry (coder-/kasm-/litellm-, dotted
# form still recognised) and upgrades CODER_ACCESS_URL to the coder name, so
# Coder builds workspace links on a secure context with a real certificate. The
# entry with no prefix is the dashboard. The names also go into
# HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS (compose appends this raw value) and into the Coder
# cert's subjectAltName.
# Requirements on the other side: the Traefik on the other box owns TLS for
# those names and must forward plain HTTP to this box's router container with
# the original Host header intact — proxy/router.conf picks the service from
# $http_host and listens on STACK_PROXY_HTTP_PORT (default 8080). A rewritten
# Host gets a 421 from the router's default server, homepage rejects the
# request, and the browser warns on the cert.
STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=
# Per-service origins bootstrap.sh writes from STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS; empty means
# LAN-only and compose falls back to STACK_LAN_HOST with the ports above.
# Point Traefik at these, not at the LAN ports.
STACK_DASHBOARD_URL=
STACK_CODER_URL=
STACK_KASM_URL=
STACK_LITELLM_URL=
# Homepage v2 login gate — anyone on the LAN who can reach 443 needs this
# password, since the dashboard lists every service in the stack. bootstrap.sh
# fills both in and prints the password once, so note it down.
HOMEPAGE_AUTH_SECRET=change-me-homepage-secret
HOMEPAGE_AUTH_PASSWORD=change-me-homepage

# --- Coder Docker access --------------------------------------------------
# GID of the host docker group. The coder container runs as uid 1000 and needs
# this group to reach the mounted /var/run/docker.sock. bootstrap.sh fills it
# in from `getent group docker`; check it after reinstalling Docker.
DOCKER_GID=980
